if / else conditions in awk
awk '{if($3>50000) print $1,"SENIOR"; else print $1,"JUNIOR"}' emp.txt
Label employees as SENIOR or JUNIOR based on salary column

awk '{if($3>80) print "A"; else if($3>60) print "B"; else print "C"}' scores.txt
Grade students — A/B/C based on score in column 3

awk '{status=($3>0)?"UP":"DOWN"; print $1, status}' servers.txt
Ternary operator — one-line if/else for server status check

awk '$3>1000 && $4=="active" {print $1}' users.txt
AND condition — print only active users with more than 1000 points

awk '$2=="admin" || $2=="root" {print $1,$2}' users.txt
OR condition — print admin or root users
Pattern matching with conditions

awk 'NF > 0 {print}' file.txt
Skip empty lines — NF=0 means no fields = empty line

awk 'NR==1 || NR==FNR {print}' file.txt
Print only first and last line of file

awk 'NR%2==0 {print}' file.txt
Print only even-numbered lines. NR%2==1 for odd lines
Pattern vs if — when to use which?

Pattern (outside braces) → simpler, faster:
awk '$3>1000 {print}' file

if (inside braces) → more complex logic needed:
awk '{if($3>1000){print "high"} else {print "low"}}' file
